+ * ONE (data_addr == 0) is the original arrangement and is preserved byte for byte:
+ * a single capability over [base, base+tot_size) is SPLIT into code | seal | data.
+ * Because __split divides one linear capability, that arrangement forces the whole
+ * domain -- image plus stack plus every global's storage -- into a single physically
+ * contiguous allocation, and the kernel module gets that from the buddy allocator,
+ * whose maximum order is then a hard ceiling on domain image size. It capped code_len
+ * at 1,376,256 bytes; the largest domain that ever ran here is 1,375,688, i.e. 568
+ * bytes under it, and two interpreter ports have never run at all.
+ *
+ * TWO regions removes the coupling rather than raising the ceiling. Nothing new is
+ * needed for it: split_out_cap already mints a capability over an arbitrary range,
+ * which is how call_domain_with_cap hands a running domain a second region. The
+ * domain sees no difference -- it still receives dom_code and dom_data through the
+ * sealed region -- so the entry glue is untouched.
